#h1{Reading the notation}

Everything starts with the grammar. A document is a tree of bags, written with a hash, a tag and
braces. A bag whose tag is not reserved defines a chunk; the reserved tags are the headings, a
citation, a bibliography, an include, an image and an agent hint. Code is any run of three or
more backticks; shorter runs are inline code and belong to the prose. The parser is the brashtag
package, compiled into the program, and a small wrapper gives it one more function, which parses
a bag and returns the rest of the input.

Four rules make the text predictable. Chunk names are global, and defining a name twice
concatenates the definitions, which is how a chunk can be explained in several places. A name
that ends in an extension is a file. For programs too large for one file,
`#include{chapter.lit}` is replaced by the text of that file, so a program can have a chapter
for each module or subsystem. And a bag called `agent-fill-in` inside a chunk is a gap: it is
not code, tangle skips it, and `lit fill` knows how to fill it. All four rules are about the
whole document, so no tool looks at a chunk in isolation, and a chunk in one chapter may refer
to a chunk in another.
